# Riverpod — Testing Patterns

## Container setup

Always use `ProviderContainer.test()` — never bare `ProviderContainer()`.
One container per test; never share between tests.

```dart
test('description', () {
  final container = ProviderContainer.test();
  expect(container.read(myProvider), 'expected value');
  addTearDown(container.dispose);
});
```

## Auto-dispose providers — use listen, not read

`container.read()` on an auto-dispose provider disposes it immediately after reading.
Use `container.listen()` to keep it alive:

```dart
final subscription = container.listen<String>(myProvider, (_, __) {});
expect(subscription.read(), 'expected value');
```

## Overriding dependencies

Override at the container boundary — never mutate global state.

```dart
final container = ProviderContainer.test(
  overrides: [
    // Function provider
    repositoryProvider.overrideWith((ref) => MockRepository()),
    // Fixed value
    configProvider.overrideWithValue(Config.test()),
    // Family — single argument
    userProvider('123').overrideWith((ref) => User(name: 'Test')),
    // Family — all arguments
    userProvider.overrideWith((ref, arg) => User(name: 'User $arg')),
  ],
);
```

## Mocking Notifiers

**Subclass, do not implement.** The mock must extend the generated base class,
not implement the public class. With code generation the mock usually needs to
be in the same file as the Notifier (to access the private generated base).

```dart
// ✅ correct
class MockCounterNotifier extends _$CounterNotifier {
  @override
  int build() => 0;
}

// ❌ wrong — breaks generated internals
class MockCounterNotifier extends Mock implements CounterNotifier {}
```

Prefer mocking a *dependency* (repository, service) over mocking the Notifier itself.

## Listening and verifying state changes

Use `ProviderListener<T>` from `test/utils.dart` together with mocktail:

```dart
import '../utils.dart'; // ProviderListener<T>

test('state changes correctly', () {
  final container = ProviderContainer.test();
  final listener = ProviderListener<String>();

  container.listen(myProvider, listener.call, fireImmediately: true);

  verifyInOrder([() => listener(null, 'initial')]);
  verifyNoMoreInteractions(listener);

  container.read(myProvider.notifier).doSomething();

  verifyInOrder([() => listener('initial', 'updated')]);
  verifyNoMoreInteractions(listener);
});
```

## Async providers

```dart
// Await the future directly
await expectLater(
  container.read(myAsyncProvider.future),
  completion('expected value'),
);

// Or read after settling
await container.read(myAsyncProvider.future);
expect(container.read(myAsyncProvider).value, 'expected value');
```

Use `FakeAsync` from `package:fake_async` when the provider uses `Timer` or `Duration`.

## Widget tests

Wrap under test in `ProviderScope`. Access the container via `tester.container()`.

```dart
testWidgets('description', (tester) async {
  await tester.pumpWidget(
    ProviderScope(
      overrides: [myProvider.overrideWith((ref) => 'test value')],
      child: const MyWidget(),
    ),
  );
  final container = tester.container();
  expect(container.read(myProvider), 'test value');
});
```

## Mocking with mocktail

```dart
class MockMyService extends Mock implements MyService {}

setUpAll(() {
  registerFallbackValue(MyServiceRequest());
});

setUp(() {
  final mock = MockMyService();
  when(() => mock.fetch(any())).thenAnswer((_) async => 'result');
});
```
